  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a4da58d2dc2409c459ef20449bf92a90, type: 3}
  m_Name: Beam
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  lessonId: beam-bridge
  lessonTitle: What is a Beam Bridge?
  lessonImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 6aedd357aa978324690d88ed82a19beb, type: 3}
  lessonDescription: 'The Beam Bridge is the oldest, simplest, and most common type
    of bridge in the world. If you have ever walked across a wooden plank laid over
    a small creek, you have used a beam bridge!


    It consists of a rigid horizontal
    structure (the beam) resting on two vertical supports (the piers) at either end.


    The
    Engineering Inside:

    When a Live Load (like a heavy truck) drives across
    a beam bridge, the weight pushes straight down on the beam. This causes two different
    forces to happen at the exact same time:


    Compression: The weight squishes
    the top of the bridge together.


    Tension: The bending causes the bottom
    of the bridge to stretch and pull apart.


    If the beam is not thick or strong
    enough to handle these forces, it will sag in the middle and eventually snap!


    The
    Challenge:

    Because beam bridges rely purely on the stiffness of the material,
    they have a major limitation: distance. A beam bridge can only stretch so far
    before it bends under its own weight.


    To cross larger rivers or canyons
    with a beam bridge, engineers must build multiple piers in the water or ground
    below to hold up the middle sections.


    Engineer''s Tip: Beam bridges are
    incredibly cheap and effective for short gaps. But if the gap is too wide and
    you cannot build piers below it, you will need a more advanced design!'
